Practical 3: Mathematical Functions

• Write a program to calculate the square root of a number.

code :-

import math

num = float(input("Enter a number: "))

if num >= 0:
    square_root = math.sqrt(num)
    print ("Square root of", num, "is", square_root) 
else:
    print("Square root of a negative number is not possible.")

output :-

Enter a number: 5 
Square root of 5.0 is 2.23606797749979

• Explore use of the math module.
The math module provides many built-in 
mathematical functions such as sqrt(), pow(), ceil(), 
floor(), factorial(), trigonometric functions, and 
logarithmic functions, making mathematical 
calculations simple and efficient in Python.

code :-

import math

num = float(input("Enter a number: "))

print("Square Root:", math.sqrt(num))
print("Power (Square):", math.pow(num, 2))
print("Ceiling Value:", math.ceil(num))
print("Floor Value:", math.floor(num))
print("Factorial of 5:", math.factorial (5))
print("Value of Pi:", math.pi)
print("Value of e:", math.e)
print("Sine of 30°:", math.sin(math.radians(30)))
print("Cosine of 60°:", math.cos (math.radians (60)))
print("Logarithm (base e):", math.log(num))

output :-

